function-values.lust:4:13  a is a do(int) function, but this is a do int(int) function. A function only fits where it takes and gives back exactly the same types.
function-values.lust:5:17  b is a do int(int) function, but this is a do(string) function. A function only fits where it takes and gives back exactly the same types.
function-values.lust:6:9  c is an int, but this is a do int(int) function. Add () to call it.
function-values.lust:8:12  s is a string, but this is an int. Use .text() to turn it into text.
function-values.lust:8:14  d's first thing is an int, but this is a string. Use .int() to turn text into a number.
function-values.lust:9:9  d takes 1 thing (int), but got 2.
function-values.lust:10:1  d(…) gives back an int, but nothing uses it. Put it in a variable: int x = …;
function-values.lust:12:9  x is an int, not something you can call.
function-values.lust:13:10  print is built into Lust, so it can't be kept as a value. Wrap it in your own function: do show(string s) { print(s); }
function-values.lust:16:13  Dog has no method called howl.
function-values.lust:17:13  hp is a field: bo.hp
function-values.lust:18:4  add () to call it: bo:bark()
function-values.lust:20:9  this is an int, not a function, so it can't be called.
